      "widgets_values": [
        "# 03 \u00b7 control/style\n\n**Read this before you use it: this endpoint keeps none of your image's composition.**\n\nIt is the odd one of the four. The image you give it is a **style reference**, not a structural one. It looks at palette, texture, mark-making and light, then generates an entirely new picture from your *prompt*, at whatever `aspect_ratio` you set. Nothing of the input's arrangement survives, and it is not supposed to.\n\nIf you wire it up expecting a restyled version of your card and get an unrelated image in roughly the right colours, it is working correctly and you wanted `control/structure` or `control/style-transfer`.\n\n## What it is actually for\n\nTwo things, both real:\n\n- **Extracting a look.** Point it at a card you have approved and prompt for something else entirely. What comes back tells you what the model thinks your style *is*, which is a genuinely useful thing to learn before you lock it across 78 cards.\n- **Generating new material in an established style** \u2014 a card back, a box panel, a border motif \u2014 where you have no composition to preserve because there is nothing there yet.\n\n## The dial\n\n`fidelity` 0-1 is how closely the output matches the reference's look.\n\n- **0.2** \u2014 the reference is a hint. Mostly the prompt's picture.\n- **0.5** \u2014 the default; a clear family resemblance.\n- **0.9** \u2014 as close to the reference's surface as it can manage, sometimes at the cost of following the prompt.\n\n`aspect_ratio` matters here in a way it does not elsewhere, because there is no input geometry to inherit. Set it to your card's ratio (`4:5` is close to standard tarot stock) or you will get squares.\n\n## Chaining these four\n\nThey all share one convention, and that is the whole mechanism \u2014 there is no workflow-calls-a-workflow in ComfyUI, so a folder is the join:\n\n```\nstability/in/              drop your sources here\nstability/out/sketch/      <- 01 writes here\nstability/out/structure/   <- 02\nstability/out/style/       <- 03\nstability/out/transfer/    <- 04\n```\n\nEvery one of these graphs reads **the newest image** in its `directory` (`sort_by = modified`, `descending`, `limit = 1`). So to feed one endpoint's output into another, change this graph's `directory` to that graph's output folder and run it. Structure reading `stability/out/sketch` is a two-step chain; nothing else needs rewiring.\n\nThe filename carries through: `Load Images From Directory` emits `filenames`, which is wired to the save node's `label`, so `the-fool.png` stays `the-fool.png` at every stage and you can always tell which source a result came from. `overwrite` is off, so a second run lands as `the-fool_1.png` rather than destroying the first \u2014 and \"newest by modified\" still picks it up.\n\nEach result gets a sidecar JSON with the prompt and settings that made it.\n\n## Cost\n\nEach of the three nodes below is **one paid call**, so a run is three. That is the point of a study graph \u2014 you are buying the comparison, not the image. **Ctrl+B** any of them to drop it from the run once you know which end of the dial you care about; bypass passes the input straight through and costs nothing.\n\nSeeds are fixed at **7**. Stability treats seed 0 as \"pick a random one\", which would make the three results incomparable and stop any of them being cached. With the seed pinned, the dial is the only thing that differs between them.\n\n## The prompt lives in one node\n\nThe **Shared Prompt** node feeds every `prompt` field in this graph \u2014 all three settings \u2014 and their `negative` too, so there is one\nplace to edit and the copies cannot drift apart. The comparison is only honest while every setting is reading the same words.\n\nIt emits a plain string, **not** a list. That is the difference between it and **Prompt\nList**, which would make each node it feeds run once per line and multiply what the graph\ncosts."
      ],
      "title": "Read me \u2014 control/style",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
